\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Non-Abelian fields}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{n=4}, #{N_1=N_2=N_3=N_4=4}, #{a=1}, let #{S} be the Pauli module of dimension #{16} with
#{\Gamma_S=C}, and let #{F=\mathbb C^2}. Take the links #{\operatorname{diag}(U^a_j(x),U^b_j(x))},
where #{U^a} and #{U^b} are fields with link angles the sums of those of
\ref{fdip-015W} in the planes #{(1,2)} and #{(3,4)}. Let #{U^a} have the fluxes
#{(m_{12},m_{34})=(1,0)} and #{U^b} the fluxes #{(0,1)}. The least computed absolute eigenvalue of #{\Gamma K_U} is positive
and #{\tfrac{1}{2}(\nu_{-}-\nu_{+})} is #{0}. Compare this field with
#{\operatorname{diag}(U^a_j(x)U^b_j(x),1)} on the same fiber. Their determinants
agree on every link and have fluxes #{(1,1)}. The first character of the comparison
field has computed index #{4} and the trivial character has index #{0}; by
\ref{fdip-015T} these give index #{4} for the comparison field.
When both #{U^a} and #{U^b} have fluxes #{(1,1)}, the computed index is #{8}, and
the determinant has fluxes #{(2,2)}. The equal-determinant fields in this finite
calculation give different index counts.}

\p{A general finite equality between a non-Abelian overlap index and a gauge-field
topological charge remains a stronger question. For a class of topologically nontrivial
fields, Adams recovers the continuum charge from the overlap index for a mass parameter
in #{(0,2)}, taking an infinite-volume limit followed by a classical continuum limit
\citet{Abstract, p. 1; Equation (5.3), p. 24}{adams1999axial}.
Lüscher relates the non-Abelian anomaly to a local lattice topological field representing
the Chern character in #{4+2} dimensions \citet{Abstract, p. 1}{luscher1999weyl}.
Kikukawa and Nakayama determine how the anomaly-related topological field depends on
an admissible #{\mathrm U(1)_Y} field with the #{\mathrm{SU}(2)_L} background fixed
\citet{Abstract, p. 1}{kikukawa2000gauge}.
For a single classical #{\mathrm{SU}(2)} instanton, Brower, Orginos and Tan find a
circular monopole trajectory in maximal Abelian projection
\citet{Abstract, p. 1}{brower1997magnetic}.
Lüscher suggests that non-Abelian field space may have more complicated topology,
making the absence of global topological obstructions difficult to prove \citet{Section 12, printed p. 43}{luscher1998abelian}.
These statements are cited, not proved here.}
